### Two trust failures
First, userId comes from req.body, so a parent can send someone else's id and book or pay as them. Second, price comes from the browser, so anyone can send price 0 and get a free class. The SQL is also built by joining strings, which allows injection.

### Corrected handler
app.post('/api/bookings', requireLogin, async (req, res) => {
  const userId = req.session.userId;
  const { slotId } = req.body;
  const slot = await db.query('SELECT id, price FROM slots WHERE id = $1 AND seats_left > 0', [slotId]);
  if (!slot.rows.length) return res.status(409).json({ error: 'slot_unavailable' });
  const saved = await db.query(
    'INSERT INTO bookings (user_id, slot_id, amount) VALUES ($1, $2, $3) RETURNING id',
    [userId, slotId, slot.rows[0].price]
  );
  res.status(201).json({ id: saved.rows[0].id });
});

### Three negative tests
test('ignores a userId sent in the body', async () => {
  const res = await api.as('priya').post('/api/bookings', { slotId: 4, userId: 99 });
  expect(await bookingOwner(res.body.id)).toBe('priya');
});
test('ignores a tampered price', async () => {
  const res = await api.as('priya').post('/api/bookings', { slotId: 4, price: 0 });
  expect(await bookingAmount(res.body.id)).toBe(500);
});
test('rejects a duplicate request', async () => {
  await api.as('priya').post('/api/bookings', { slotId: 4, key: 'k1' });
  const again = await api.as('priya').post('/api/bookings', { slotId: 4, key: 'k1' });
  expect(again.status).toBe(409);
});

### Where the price comes from
A slots table on the server, looked up by slot id

### Your reasoning
I took userId from the session because the browser can send any number, and I looked up the price in the slots table because the form field can be edited in dev tools. The negative tests prove the server ignores those fields, which matters more than a nice-looking page.

