### Test to automate first
The last-seat boundary (nine of ten taken)

### Three test cases
test('books a seat when 3 of 10 are taken', () => {
  expect(canBook(3, 10)).toBe(true);
});
test('allows the last seat when 9 of 10 are taken', () => {
  expect(canBook(9, 10)).toBe(true);
});
test('refuses when all 10 seats are taken', () => {
  expect(canBook(10, 10)).toBe(false);
});

### Boundary test name
Last seat: nine of ten taken still lets the tenth parent book

### Fixed seat function
function canBook(taken, capacity) {
  // taken = seats already booked in this batch
  return taken < capacity;
}

### Your reasoning
I would automate the nine-of-ten case first because the last seat is where off-by-one mistakes hide and it protects the main promise of the app. The old code used capacity - 1, so the tenth parent was always refused. The normal and failure tests alone would have passed anyway.

